sections.body:
Every HTML document served from `napp://` receives a small script before `</head>` that defines `window.napp`. Read it instead of guessing the user's settings.

```js
const { language, theme, accent, accentColor, repositoryUrl } = window.napp;
document.documentElement.dataset.nappTheme = theme;
```

The runtime also sets `data-napp-theme`, `data-napp-language` and `color-scheme` on `<html>`, and publishes CSS custom properties:

```css
body {
  background: var(--napp-bg);
  color: var(--napp-text);
  accent-color: var(--napp-accent);
}
```

Available variables: `--napp-accent`, `--napp-bg`, `--napp-surface`, `--napp-surface-alt`, `--napp-border`, `--napp-text`, `--napp-muted`.

`window.napp` is frozen. It reflects the launcher settings at launch time; restart the app to pick up a later change.

Host routes live under `napp://<appId>/__host/` and never touch the package files.

## Export a file

```js
const response = await fetch("/__host/export/hello.ipkg", {
  method: "POST",
  body: bytes,
});
const { path, name, bytes: written } = await response.json();
```

The host sanitises the file name, writes atomically into the app's `downloads` folder, avoids overwriting an existing file (`name (2).ext`) and rejects bodies above 64 MB. This is the supported way to produce a download: the Chromium download manager cannot choose a safe target inside the sandboxed app.

## Session info

```js
const info = await (await fetch("/__host/info")).json();
```

Returns the same object as `window.napp`, including `permissions` and `runtimeVersion`.

